A Hypergraph draws itself by default, the way a Graph does, so HypergraphPlot \
is needed only in order to pass options.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"876c4e75-4adc-2141-9578-40a095ecf104"],

Cell["Arrays", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"bd91b7b5-8d72-f54c-bbc6-64013c958209"],

Cell["\<\
An array is a nesting of lists whose entries either take values in a \
numerical domain or are generated symbolically from a name. The two are \
different objects. The order of an array is its number of indices, and an \
array of order n is an n-array: a vector is a 1-array and a matrix is a \
2-array. A scalar is the 0-array, with no indices and a single entry. An \
array is regular when all of its indices range over the same length, and only \
a regular array can carry an index symmetry.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"45dfd1f9-81e6-d149-a642-4246b6690792"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["NumericalArray", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "8a91ca82-654b-2f44-a983-aa6d8636ef7a"],
 " \[LongDash] ",
 "an array with explicit entries, drawn from a value domain"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7cccde23-2ba0-5c4c-b15c-6060af3b032b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["SymbolicArray", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "bbfa5328-1d84-5849-8de3-2677fc97a6ec"],
 " \[LongDash] ",
 "an array whose entries are generated from a name and the indices"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f5f51303-1dc2-0c45-90cb-98a45553e610"],

